speechtext:
I do not rise to attack the bonus system in detail or to object to any one particular item. I merely wish to urge the suggestion that was made in the early part of the session : That in this great question, in which is involved the question of transportation, it would be greatly in the interests of the country if when these subsidies are to be voted, the members in this House and the people of the country should have confidence that that money is going to be used for a useful purpose-that the road to be subsidized is in the general interest of the country, rather than, as my hon. friend says, merely a I'oad projected for the pur-

pose of getting a charter and getting subsidies from this House year after year, thus deterring more useful railroads from being built. The suggestion that was made was that a commission should be appointed, composed of men who would be experts, and who would view the whole question from a point of view away and above any local interest; so that when an application for a charter came before this House or before a committee of this House, the members would have something to go upon. It is within the knowledge of every hon. gentleman on the Railway Committee of this House that charters are applied for which many members cannot and do not pretend to understand, and are voted on almost blindly. But if such applications were reported upon by a commission composed of men like our present Auditor General, who would be above suspicion of any interest in pushing the charter, I believe millions of money would be saved to this country, and the country would ultimately be benefited by having a uniform system of transportation. It is very difficult to say what form this commission should take ; but I think the talent in this government, with such men as the Minister of Railways and the Minister of Public Works, who has shown such an interest in the development of the country, more particularly in the development of our great waterway, the St. Lawrence river, of which we are all so proud, should be equal to such a task. Such a commission would inspire confidence in the people of this country generally, and its reports would enable us to vote money intelligently in the general interest of the country, instead of following a sort of go-as-you-please policy, as we have been doing for years-voting first a small subsidy, which is made a lever in succeeding sessions to get the subsidy doubled or quadrupled, until the country becomes involved in very large expenditures, as in the case of the Trent Valley canal, which I think every intelligent man in this country not actually situated on its banks, knows to have been a great waste of public money. I would therefore take advantage of the present opportunity to urge on the government to think seriously of the suggestion which has been made, and not to refuse to consider it because it comes from this side of the House. Let them give us credit, as well as their own friends, of being able to rise above party politics in a matter of such serious consequence to the country at large. Many of these grants are revotes, it is true, and the money will never be earned ; but they stand as deterrents to more useful enterprises. We have heard recently of a charter-monger who sold a railway charter for $00,000, and then turned round and applied for another running almost parallel to the one he sold. I contend that such a thing as that could be obviated if the government would appoint a commission of the charae-172}
ter I have referred to ; and if they do that, they will establish a record in this country which I believe will not have been equalled by that of any government that went before them. The great question you have to consider is not how you will manage to maintain yourselves in power, but how you will benefit the great interests of this country ; and you can do that by giving your serious consideration to the great transportation problem, and the mode in which we are going to get to the markets of the world in the most economical way the natural products of this country, which are increasing by leaps and bounds. I would ask the government to take this matter into consideration, if not at the present time, then during the recess ; and I trust that before we meet again they will have for the consideration of this House and this country a recommendation for a commission which will be able to deal efficiently with the very question we are now discussing in such a way as to command the confidence of the whole Dominion.
